export const SYLLABUS_MARKDOWN: string = "# Human-Centered Artificial Intelligence \u2014 2026\n\nInstructor: Chat Wacharamanotham (to email, see OLAT) Office hours:\n<https://chatw.ch/h>\\\nCourse format: Lecture with in-class exercises\n\n## Course description\n\nArtificial Intelligence (AI) is now a building block of computing\nsystems. When developers of AI-powered systems change their algorithms\nor training data, the system could change its behavior wildly. This\ndynamic nature makes designing the user experiences of AI-powered\nsystems more difficult than deterministic systems. Beyond the immediate\nusers of such systems, the behavior of the AI-powered systems may impact\nthe lives of other people and society at large. Hence, designing systems\nwith AI is a broader perspective.\n\nIn this course, you will learn about the capabilities and limitations of\nvarious current AI systems. You will also learn about desirable\nqualities of AI systems, e.g., effectiveness, fairness, and ethics. You\nwill also learn processes and techniques for designing AI-powered\nsystems. Finally, you will learn about AI's current and potential\nimpacts on users and society and how to study them.\n\n## Intended learning outcomes\n\nStudents can explain the capabilities and limitations of current AI\nsystems and their implications for interaction design.\n\nStudents understand and can critically analyze AI systems in their\nethics, fairness, accountability, and transparency aspects.\n\nStudents can apply a human-centered design process to design the user\nexperience of systems with AI components.\n\nStudents can explain the implications of AI applications to stakeholders\nand society\u2014beyond the immediate users.\n\nStudents can identify research methods to study HCI in AI-powered\nsystems\n\n## Prior knowledge\n\nExperiences with the following topics can be helpful, but they are not\nrequirements:\n\nHuman\u2013Computer Interaction (HCI), e.g., Human-Centered Design process,\nUsability study methods\n\nBasic programming or computational thinking skills(e.g., functions,\niterations, recursions)\n\n## Workload\n\n6 ECTS = ~180 hours of work (including classes, assignments, project,\nreviews, and the exam)\n\n## Learning resources\n\nReading materials will be made available on OLAT. There's no required\ntextbook for this course.\n\n## Semester plan\n\nLectures are in room BIN-0-K.02 on Wednesdays 16:15\u201318:00 and Thursdays\n8:00\u20139:45. The following table gives an overview. The course plan page\non OLAT is the latest version. It also has further details, such as\nreading homework.\n\nWeek 1 \u2014 **Wed. Sep 16** \u2014 Course introduction and capabilities of Large Language Models. Project: Brainstorm potential user groups. Assignment: Start: System choices and justification.\nWeek 1 \u2014 **Thu. Sep 17** \u2014 Course introduction and capabilities of Large Language Models.\nWeek 2 \u2014 **Wed. Sep 23** \u2014 Framework for decoding AI news.\nWeek 2 \u2014 **Thu. Sep 24** \u2014 Framework for decoding AI news. Assignment: Due: System choices and justification.\nWeek 3 \u2014 **Wed. Sep 30** \u2014 Design principles for interactive systems. Project: Form project team.\nWeek 3 \u2014 **Thu. Oct 1** \u2014 Design principles for interactive systems. Project: Due: Team registration.\nWeek 4 \u2014 **Wed. Oct 7** \u2014 Extending design principles to HCAI systems. Project: First meeting. Assignment: First draft.\nWeek 4 \u2014 **Thu. Oct 8** \u2014 Extending design principles to HCAI systems. Project: Due: User group proposals.\nWeek 5 \u2014 **Wed. Oct 14** \u2014 Methods for understanding people and designing for them. Project: Work on understanding users + task analysis. Assignment: Due: Design analysis draft.\nWeek 5 \u2014 **Thu. Oct 15** \u2014 Methods for understanding people and designing for them. Assignment: Revise.\nWeek 6 \u2014 **Wed. Oct 21** \u2014 Fairness, accountability, and ethical data.\nWeek 6 \u2014 **Thu. Oct 22** \u2014 Fairness, accountability, and ethical data. Project: Due: Task analysis.\nWeek 7 \u2014 **Wed. Oct 28** \u2014 (Project input). Assignment: Due: Design analysis final.\nWeek 7 \u2014 **Thu. Oct 29** \u2014 (Project input). Project: Work on two candidates of design prototypes.\nWeek 8 \u2014 **Wed. Nov 4** \u2014 Explainability.\nWeek 8 \u2014 **Thu. Nov 5** \u2014 Explainability.\nWeek 9 \u2014 **Wed. Nov 11** \u2014 Ethics in HCAI.\nWeek 9 \u2014 **Thu. Nov 12** \u2014 Ethics in HCAI.\nWeek 10 \u2014 **Wed. Nov 18** \u2014 No class.\nWeek 10 \u2014 **Thu. Nov 19** \u2014 No class. Project: Due: Two prototypes.\nWeek 11 \u2014 **Wed. Nov 25** \u2014 Peer feedback for the project \u00b7 AI in knowledge work and education.\nWeek 11 \u2014 **Thu. Nov 26** \u2014 Peer feedback for the project \u00b7 AI in knowledge work and education. Project: Polish one design prototype and prepare the project report.\nWeek 12 \u2014 **Wed. Dec 2** \u2014 No class.\nWeek 12 \u2014 **Thu. Dec 3** \u2014 (Project input).\nWeek 13 \u2014 **Wed. Dec 9** \u2014 No class.\nWeek 13 \u2014 **Thu. Dec 10** \u2014 Exam preparation. Project: Due: Prototype and report.\nWeek 14 \u2014 **Wed. Dec 16** \u2014 Project oral exam.\nWeek 14 \u2014 **Thu. Dec 17** \u2014 Project oral exam.\n**Wed. Dec 23** \u2014 Exam preparation.\nWeek 15 \u2014 **Wed. Jan 13** \u2014 Exam is at room BIN-0-K.0.\n\n## Assessments\n\n20% Design analysis assignment\n\n50% Project\n\n30% Written exam\n\n**The design analysis assignment** is a solo work. You will pick a\nsystem and analyze it with the design principles that you will learn in\nthis course. Your first draft will receive peer feedback in class. You\nwill then revise and hand in the final version. The feedback on this\nwork will be staggered, see Policies below.\n\n**The project** is a design of an HCAI system. You will work in teams of\n5\u20136 members. You will build prototype(s) and write a report. Afterward,\nyou will defend your project in an oral exam. Some groups may develop a\nsoftware prototype as part of their project, but a successful project\ndoes not necessarily require programming. When you hand in the project\nmaterials, you will also hand in a rating of the contributions of each\nteam member[^1]. The project material will receive a team score. This\nscore will be adjusted based on the aggregated contribution ratings.\n\nThe oral exam will ask questions about your work. The question will be\ndirected to a member of your team. If they cannot answer that question,\nthe whole team will be penalized. For this part, you sink or swim\ntogether. The whole team will receive the same score.\n\n**The written exam** is pen-and-paper, and no aids are allowed. It will\nbe a mix of single-choice questions and open questions. The exam's\ncontent is drawn from all lecture slides (except slides explicitly\nmarked as not on the exam) and all reading homework.\n\n## Policies\n\n**Participation and attendance:** We will not check your attendance. You\nare responsible for catching up with materials and announcements in\nclass. In the past, students who came to class were more successful in\nfinding a project team and performed better in the exam.\n\n**Learning accommodations** with respect to disability: see information\nfrom the faculty[^2]\n\n- **Feedback vs. assessment:** Before you hand in your work for grading,\n  we organize various occasions for you to receive *formative feedback*\n  from classmates and the instructor. After the grading, you will\n  receive an *assessment* based on a set of criteria. Due to a large\n  number of students, the feedback after grading is likely to be less\n  rich than the formative feedback.\n\n- **Assignment hand-ins** are on OLAT under the \u201cHand in\u201d section in the\n  folder \u201cParticipant Drop Box\u201d. There will be a sub-folder for each\n  deliverable.\n\n  - **Late hand-in:** If you upload the file after the deadline, email\n    Chat. Your work will receive a penalty based on how late you were.\n    Any work handed in more than 12 hours after the deadline will not be\n    graded.\n\n  <!-- -->\n\n  - When the **feedback** is released, it will be in the \u201cHand in\u201d\n    section, in the \u201cCoach Return Box\u201d folder. We recommend that you\n    turn on notifications for this folder. (See the *Notification*\n    section below.)\n\n    - **Staggered feedback:** Although we want to give feedback as soon\n      as possible, our staff capacity is limited. Therefore, for some\n      assignments, some students will receive feedback earlier than\n      others. To ensure fairness across project teams, we will stratify\n      feedback by team. This means that at least one person in your\n      group will receive feedback in each batch.\n\n  <!-- -->\n\n  - **Appeal:** Within one week of receiving the assessment, you may\n    file a written appeal on [this\n    page](https://uzhwwf.qualtrics.com/jfe/form/SV_b9Dzs7f5A4J2hTM).\n    Your appeal will be processed at the end of the semester, and you\n    will receive the results along with the course\u2019s final grade.\n\n    **Rules for AI tools:** You may use any AI tools. No need to hand in\n    any disclosure. However, you are accountable for any errors in the\n    work you handed in.\n\n    Work containing **plagiarism** will not receive any score. Please\n    read the plagiarism fact sheet[^3].\n\n- **Verifiable citation requirement:** Citations in your written work\n  submitted during the semester must refer to a source that exists. A\n  source exists if it can be confirmed by any of the following methods:\n\n  - a publicly accessible academic database,\n\n  - a library catalog entry, a verifiable ISBN/DOI, or\n\n  - a stable URL accompanied by a timestamped archived copy (e.g., via\n    the Wayback Machine or a similar archival service) submitted\n    alongside the work, confirming accessibility at or before the time\n    of submission.\n\n> If a source cannot be verified, this mistake will be taken into\n> consideration for the grading of the written submission in question.\n> This requirement applies to the final graded submission (not to\n> drafts).\n\n**Communication:** Please ask questions that do not contain sensitive\ninformation on the OLAT forum \u201cCourse Q&A\u201d. For questions that can be\nresolved quickly in a conversation, please visit the office hours\n(<https://chatw.ch/h>). If you need to email, use the OLAT \u201cEmail\ninstructor\u201d to ensure you provide complete information. We will\nprioritize answering questions on the forum; emails will be processed\nlater.\n\n**Notification:** We expect you to receive notifications from the course\nOLAT\u2019s \u201cNotifications\u201d page. Please click the bell icon and choose\n\u201cSubscribe\u201d. This action will send you emails of each notification. You\ncan adjust email frequency by clicking on your profile icon at the\ntop-right and choosing \u201cSystem settings\u201d.\n\n**Grading scale interpretation:**\n\n> 6.00 Excellent\n>\n> 5.75 \u2013 5.50 Very good\n>\n> 5.25 \u2013 5.00 Good\n>\n> 4.75 \u2013 4.50 Satisfactory\n>\n> 4.25 \u2013 4.00 Sufficient\n>\n> 3.75 \u2013 3.50 Insufficient\n>\n> 3.25 \u2013 3.00 Poor\n>\n> 2.75 \u2013 2.00 Very poor\n>\n> 1.00 Did not hand in\n>\n> 80% of past students received a grade between 4.75 and 5.50\n\n## Acknowledgement\n\nMany materials were drawn from courses by [Daniel M.\nRussell](https://sites.google.com/site/dmrussell/) and [Peter\nNorvig](https://norvig.com), [Iris\nHowley](https://www.cs.williams.edu/~iris/)\u2019s course, and [Cori\nFaklaris](https://corifaklaris.com)'s courses.\n\n[^1]: For explanation on contribution rating, see\n    <https://chatw.ch/files/grading_teamwork_example.html>.\n\n[^2]: <https://oec.uzh.ch/en/studies/petitions/disability.html>\n\n[^3]: <https://www.ifi.uzh.ch/dam/jcr:1b564225-e6bd-437c-9e20-a07bca1c4df2/Merkblatt-Plagiate_en.pdf>\n";
